msgid "`cir.vec.shuffle.dynamic` (::cir::VecShuffleDynamicOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12250
msgid "_Shuffle a vector using indices in another vector_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12259
msgid ""
"The `cir.vec.shuffle.dynamic` operation implements the undocumented form of "
"Clang's __builtin_shufflevector, where the indices of the shuffled result "
"can be runtime values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12263
msgid ""
"There are two input vectors, which must have the same number of elements. "
"The second input vector must have an integral element type. The elements of "
"the second vector are interpreted as indices into the first vector. The "
"result vector is constructed by taking the elements from the first input "
"vector from the indices indicated by the elements of the second vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "`indices`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "vector of integer type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12295
msgid "`cir.vec.splat` (::cir::VecSplatOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12297
msgid "_Convert a scalar into a vector or matrix_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12305
msgid ""
"The `cir.vec.splat` operation creates a vector or matrix value from a scalar "
"value. All elements of the result have the same value, that of the given "
"scalar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12309
msgid ""
"It's a separate operation from `cir.vec.create` because more efficient LLVM "
"IR can be generated for it, and because some optimization and analysis "
"passes can benefit from knowing that all elements of the vector have the "
"same value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:0
msgid "vector or matrix type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12340
msgid "`cir.vec.ternary` (::cir::VecTernaryOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12342
msgid "_The `cond ? a : b` ternary operator for vector types_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12351
msgid ""
"The `cir.vec.ternary` operation represents the C/C++ ternary operator, `?:`, "
"for vector types, which does a `select` on individual elements of the "
"vectors. Unlike a regular `?:` operator, there is no short circuiting. All "
"three arguments are always evaluated.  Because there is no short circuiting, "
"there are no regions in this operation, unlike cir.ternary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12357
msgid ""
"The first argument is a vector of integral type. The second and third "
"arguments are vectors of the same type and have the same number of elements "
"as the first argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12361
msgid ""
"The result is a vector of the same type as the second and third arguments. "
"Each element of the result is `(bool)a[n] ? b[n] : c[n]`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12386
msgid "`cir.vtable.address_point` (::cir::VTableAddrPointOp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12388
msgid "_Get the vtable (global variable) address point_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12399
msgid ""
"The `vtable.address_point` operation retrieves the \"effective\" address "
"(address point) of a C++ virtual table. An object internal `__vptr` gets "
"initializated on top of the value returned by this operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12403
msgid ""
"`address_point.index` (vtable index) provides the appropriate vtable within "
"the vtable group (as specified by Itanium ABI), and `address_point.offset` "
"(address point index) the actual address point within that vtable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../CIR/CIRDialect.md:12407
msgid "The return type is always `!cir.vptr`."
msgstr ""

